UNIT ts_sig_of
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@7f015c4d1023 cli/lang-ts.mts sigOf (and its helper matchClose) — the code is the truth, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG ts_sig_of(sig: text) -> record[exportName: text | none, fnType: text]
ENVELOPE sig is any well-formed text (no lone surrogate). Most inputs are TypeScript SIG lines; any other text is in scope
  and either reads (the reader does not validate TypeScript, R11) or throws (R10). Throws are observations; the message is
  not graded.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Read a ts language file's SIG line — "export function NAME<GENERICS>(PARAMS): RET" or the same with
  "export default function" — into the name it exports (none for a default export) and the function TYPE it declares,
  GENERICS + PARAMS + " => " + RET, which the ts conformance check pastes into a compiled shim. The reader refuses a SIG
  that could be more than one declaration, that names any or never, or that reaches a value's type through typeof,
  import or infer; brackets are matched on a copy of the text in
  which string literal types are blanked out. Every piece of the result is copied verbatim from the input.
BEHAVIOR
R1 BLANK means exactly the two characters U+0020 SPACE and U+0009 TAB; it is the only whitespace this unit knows outside
  R9's emptiness test. Let S be sig with every leading and every trailing BLANK removed (nothing else is trimmed: a line
  break, U+00A0 or U+FEFF at either end stays). Positions, cuts and counts below are in S, by character.
R2 HEAD: S must begin with the literal "export", one or more BLANK, optionally the literal "default" followed by one or
  more BLANK, the literal "function", one or more BLANK, then NAME, then a possibly empty run of BLANK, taken as long as
  possible. Literals are case-sensitive and must be followed by BLANK ("exportfunction", "export function" and
  "export async function" have no HEAD). NAME = one character of A-Z a-z _ $, then every following character of A-Z a-z
  0-9 _ $ (longest run: "café" gives NAME "caf" and the cursor on "é"). No HEAD -> throws. The cursor C is placed right
  after HEAD.
R3 MASK: M is S with the interior of every string literal replaced by spaces, one space per character, so M and S align
  position for position. Literals are found scanning S from the start: at a character " or ' the scan tries a literal
  closed by the next same quote character, where a backslash consumes itself and the character after it (so an escaped
  quote does not close) unless that character is one of the four BREAKS LF, CR, U+2028, U+2029; a BREAK that does not
  follow a backslash is ordinary literal content. When it closes, both quotes stay, the interior becomes spaces and the
  scan resumes after the closing quote. When it cannot close (the end of S, a backslash at the end, or a backslash before
  a BREAK), that quote is ordinary text and the scan resumes at the very next character — which may itself open
  a literal. The other quote character inside a literal is ordinary content. Every structural test below (R4 to R8) reads
  M (R4 reads T, built from M); every cut is taken from S.
R4 ONE DECLARATION: T is M with every ";" that lies at BRACE DEPTH above zero replaced by a space (an object type's
  member separator). The depth is counted over M from its start: "{" adds one, "}" subtracts one but never goes below
  zero, and a ";" is replaced when the depth from the characters before it is above zero. Braces are not required to
  balance: an extra "}" leaves the depth at zero (so a later ";" is top level unless a later "{" opens again), and an
  unclosed "{" leaves every later ";" inside. Braces inside string literals are blanked in M and never count. T containing
  any of the following throws: ";" (so only a top-level ";" refuses), a backtick, CR, LF, U+2028, U+2029, the pairs "//",
  "/*" or "*/", or an "=" whose next character is not ">" (so "=>" is allowed, while "=", "==>", ">=" and a final "="
  throw, inside braces too). Inside a string literal all of these are content (a raw CR, LF, U+2028 or U+2029 between
  quotes is masked and passes); "," is not refused. T is used by R4 alone.
R5 NO ANY, NEVER, TYPEOF, IMPORT OR INFER: M containing any of the five words "any", "never", "typeof", "import" or
  "infer" throws, where a word occurrence is one whose preceding
  character (if there is one) is not A-Z a-z 0-9 _ $ or "." and whose following character (if any) is not A-Z a-z 0-9 _ $.
  So "x.any", "$never", "company", "x.typeof", "importer" and "infers" pass, while ". any", "{ any: s }", "éany",
  "(a: typeof x)", "import('m').T", "infer U" and a NAME that is one of the five words throw; inside a string literal
  the word is blanked and passes CITED FIDELITY package.
R6 GENERICS: when M has "<" at C, find its CLOSE by counting over M from C: each "<" adds one; each ">" subtracts one
  unless the character before it is "=" (the ">" of an arrow is skipped, neither counted nor closing); the first counted
  ">" that brings the count to zero is the close. None before the end -> throws. GENERICS = the text of S from "<" through
  the close, inclusive (an empty "<>" counts); C moves past it. Otherwise GENERICS is empty and C stays.
R7 C then skips BLANK characters only (a U+00A0 or any other character before "(" is not skipped).
R8 PARAMS: M must have "(" at C (the end of S does not) -> otherwise throws. Find its close the R6 way over "(" and ")"
  alone in M (every "(" adds one, every ")" subtracts one, the first ")" reaching zero closes); none -> throws. PARAMS = the
  text of S from that "(" through that ")", inclusive.
R9 REST = the text of S after PARAMS with its leading BLANK removed. REST must begin with ":", and what follows the ":"
  must hold at least one character outside the whitespace set WS (pinned below) -> otherwise throws (an empty REST, a REST
  that begins with anything else, and a ":" followed only by WS characters such as U+000B, U+00A0 or U+FEFF all throw).
  RET = what follows that ":" with its leading BLANK removed; leading non-BLANK whitespace, U+0085 and any interior text
  ("," or a second ":") are kept.
R10 Every refusal of R2, R4, R5, R6, R8 and R9 is a throw; which one fires first affects only the message, which is not
  graded. The real messages begin "SIG is not an exported function declaration", "SIG is more than one declaration",
  "SIG uses any or never", "SIG uses typeof / import / infer", "unbalanced <>", "SIG has no parameter list", "unbalanced ()" and "SIG declares no return
  type".
R11 Result: exportName = none when HEAD held "default", else NAME; fnType = GENERICS, then PARAMS, then the four
  characters " => ", then RET — taken from S, so string literal contents are kept exactly. Nothing else is checked: NAME
  is not reserved ("export function default(): s" reads), and PARAMS and RET are never parsed as TypeScript CITED FIDELITY
  package.
PINNED charset WS = U+0009 U+000A U+000B U+000C U+000D U+0020 U+00A0 U+1680 U+2000-U+200A U+2028 U+2029 U+202F U+205F U+3000 U+FEFF CITED R9 — the emptiness test of RET only; U+0085, U+180E, U+200B and U+2060 are NOT in it
INVARIANT every non-throwing result's fnType contains ") => ", starts with "(" or "<", and is free of CR, LF, U+2028,
  U+2029, a backtick and the five R5 words outside string literals, and of any ";" at brace depth zero (R4).
ORACLE value -> oracles/ts_sig_of.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 b7af1ff5d882a1c38e1e295315e7798a8886bca35249f5a5a3359cb511ca0f71
SCOPE in contract: the whole function. Out of contract: the caller (prepareTs) that writes fnType into a conformance shim
  and runs the compiler, and the artifact any-ban.
NOTE the caller, not this unit, also holds the conformance CANARY (round 5b): prepareTs pastes fnType as a type alias
  "type RdvSig = fnType;" and requires that a function of a foreign unique-symbol type NOT be assignable to it (under
  @ts-expect-error), so a SIG that accepts nearly anything fails the check there. sigOf neither builds nor tests it.
PROVENANCE rederive@7f015c4d1023 cli/lang-ts.mts lines 132-157 (sigOf, matchClose), MIT; re-cut 2026-10-27 (round 5b delta:
  U+2028/U+2029 and typeof/import/infer refusals, #114; round 5k delta: a ";" inside braces is a member separator, #158) by
  the sighted decomposer for REDERIVE_SELF_PLAN Tier 3.
